### **Compare It! Color Indications and Observations**

#### **1. Color Code Reference Table**

| Type | Color / Background | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Same** | White background | Text is identical in both files |
| **Source only** | Green text, light green background | Text exists only in the source file |
| **Target only** | Red text | Text exists only in the target file |
| **Changed** | Blue text | Text differs between the two files |
| **Virtual line** | Gray | Placeholder line used to align differences |
| **Same in line** | Blue text | Matching portions within a compared line |
| **Change in line** | Red text | Differing portions within a compared line |
| **Merged** | Black text, sky-blue background | Merged content or merge-related display |
| **Syntax keyword** | Blue | Programming-language keywords |
| **Syntax comment** | Gray | Comments in source code |
| **Syntax number/string** | Red | Numeric values and string literals |
| **Syntax preprocessor** | Blue | Preprocessor directives |

---

#### **2. Important Distinctions**

* **Source only (green):** The text occurs exclusively in the source file.
* **Target only (red):** The text occurs exclusively in the target file.
* **Changed (blue):** The corresponding text differs between the two files.
* *Note:* "Source" and "target" refer to the two file panes and do not necessarily imply original and modified unless loaded in that specific order.

